the nominees for Best Performance by an  actress and a leading role in a play  Lauren read laughs children of a lesser  God the first time that I was introduced  to children of a lesser God was when I  was 8 the movie came out and my parents  decided to take me to see the movie even  though they knew it was for adults right  it had a huge impact on me to see  another deaf adult on the big screen I  mean I never once thought that I would  be Sarah first of all I'm a woman of  color and I just assumed that it was a  given that Sarah was going to be white  I was a school teacher so I taught  kindergarten and first grade and public  education every day I had to read aloud  with my students and that always  involves acting in a way and a tough  audience children and then I left to  raise my own two boys Kenny was  presented with the possibility of doing  a revival of children of a lesser God  and he decided that he wanted to meet a  person who was deaf first so he  contacted me we set up a time to meet  and then we set up a plan to meet once a  week for a year and we would talk about  Deaf culture I would teach him some  signs so after a year of meeting and I  was his teacher and he was my student he  then asked me to be a participant in the  first reading with Joshua Jackson and  after that reading was over he pulled me  aside and said if this goes all the way  are you willing to go all the way and my  answer is pretty clear  nope no here no speak no see no hands  and working with Joshua Jackson made the  whole process so easy he's such a  veteran he's been in this industry for  so long I was always expected to learn  to speak but I don't speak I don't do  things that I can't do well this story  holds such a special place for the deaf  community you know it opened doors to  Broadway back when it first was  performed  we definitely have a long way to go  regarding inclusivity in this business  when you hear the word inclusion in this  industry it tends to focus on color 